; Cranelisp Ring 4Q — Sprint 59: Stabilisation + dual-path persistence collapse

; Sprint 59 clears the Sprint 58 carried failures and collapses the divergent
; cache-restore and fresh-compile paths onto one `register_module` recursion.
; Highlights: docstring separator fix, AST test-form scan, restart persistence.

; Defect 3 — /sig output for a defn with a docstring now uses `-` (dash) per
; repl/spec.md §1.1, joining classification and docstring on the universal
; line. Before S59 the separator was `;` (an internal field collision).

(defn add "Add two integers." [:Int x :Int y] (+ x y))

/sig add

; Defect 8 — test-form scanning now walks `defn` bodies. Before S59,
; `program_uses_test_forms` at src/session_v4.rs scanned only TopLevel::Expr;
; a defn lexically referencing `trace` / `run-test` slipped past, and the
; extern was never added to codegen_extra_symbols — JIT finalize failed with
; "can't resolve symbol". After S59 the extern registers and the defn compiles.

(defn inspect-sum "Traces a small sum so the body references the trace extern." [] (trace (+ 1 2)))

/sig inspect-sum

(inspect-sum)

; Workstream A — dual-path persistence collapse. The `.cranelisp-cache/`
; restore path and the fresh-compile path now share one register_module
; recursion. Imports persist across REPL restarts via user.cl + cache.
; First session: create a helper module and import it.

/sh printf '(defn helper-val [] 99)\n' > helper.cl

(import [helper [helper-val]])

(helper-val)

/quit

; Restart — the fresh REPL restores user.cl (which now carries the import)
; and rehydrates the helper module through the single register_module path.
; Before S59: either "undefined variable: +" on prelude restore, or
; `helper-val` unresolved. After S59: both work on the first prompt.

(+ 40 2)

(helper-val)

/sh ls .cranelisp-cache | head -4

; Sprint 59 lands 0 carried failures on the docstring, test-form, and
; persistence axes — one code path for cache and fresh compile, one
; separator in display output, one AST traversal for test-form detection.
